steno - Plover's typing engine in C, for the Moonlander
=======================================================

SYNOPSIS

    steno [-qv] [-c CONFIG] [-p PORT]  read the keyboard, type into X
    steno -T                           paper tape
    steno -S                           suggestions
    steno -TS                          both, side by side
    steno -l WORD|OUTLINE              look it up
    steno -a OUTLINE TRANSLATION       add a stroke
    steno -A                           add strokes, asking for each
    steno -t [-c CONFIG]               strokes from stdin, print what
                                       would be typed (for tests)

DESCRIPTION

steno does what Plover does while you type, and nothing else: it reads
strokes from the Moonlander's Gemini PR serial port, looks them up in
Dotterel's dictionaries and types the text into X. It follows Plover
5.4.2's own code step by step (translator, formatter, orthography), so
the same strokes give the same text; on random corpora of 9,000
strokes the output is the same as Plover's, byte for byte.

It is one static binary (about 5 MB, Plover's word list included) and
needs nothing but the kernel and a running X: no Python, no Qt, no
libX11 (it speaks the X protocol itself, through XTEST). It starts in
about 0.06 s and uses about 13 MB, where Plover takes about 0.7 s and
110 MB; a stroke takes a few microseconds.

What Plover's plugins did for Dotterel is built in:

    Gemini PR, reconnecting by itself when the keyboard comes back
    plover-modal-dictionary (.modal files)
    dotterel-modifiers.py (KHR, SH, THRA, KPW and the rest)
    plover-stitching, plover-last-translation ({*+}, {*!} ...)
    Dotterel's raw mode (RA*U) and UK/US switch (#SU)

The dictionaries are its own: ./install.sh steno clones the dotterel
repo into ~/.system/share/steno/dotterel and updates it every run.
steno never reads Plover's files, and the two don't depend on each
other.

Dotterel's other tools (Builder, Practice, Stats ...) are not, on
purpose: they stay in Plover.

Run only one of Plover and steno: both would read the same keyboard.

    steno            in a terminal: types, and shows the status screen
    steno &          in the background: types, and says nothing but
                     problems, on stderr
    steno -v         also say each stroke

STATUS SCREEN

Started in the foreground of a terminal, steno shows what it is doing
in a small screen, in the look of btop:

    +-+steno+-------------------------------------------------------+
    | keyboard    connected  /dev/ttyACM0                           |
    | X           connected  :0                                     |
    | doing       typing                                            |
    | last        KAT  cat                                          |
    | since start 212 strokes, 10 dictionaries, 130831 entries      |
    +--+q quit+-+o output+------------------------------------------+
    +-+dictionaries+------------------------------------------------+
    |> 1 on  ~/.system/share/steno/user.json    3  json, -a adds .. |
    |  2 on  dotterel-base.json            113816  json             |
    |  3 off dotterel-affixes.json            602  json             |
    |  ...                                                          |
    +--+j/k choose+-+J/K move+-+space on/off+-+a add+-+d remove+----+
    +-+messages+----------------------------------------------------+
    | keyboard on /dev/ttyACM0                                      |
    +---------------------------------------------------------------+

"doing" is typing, output off, raw mode, movement mode (Dotterel's
.modal dictionary) or nothing (no keyboard). The messages are what
steno would say on stderr.

    j k, Up Down   choose a dictionary (g, G: the first, the last)
    J K, Shift+Up Shift+Down
                   move it up or down: the higher one wins
    space          turn it on or off (in raw mode: for when it ends)
    a              add one: a name in the dictionaries directory, or
                   a path; it goes last (Enter adds, Esc stops)
    d              take it out of the list (y to be sure); the file
                   stays
    r              read again the dictionary files that changed
    o              output on or off, as PLOVER:TOGGLE
    q, Ctrl+C      quit steno

Every change to the list is written to the settings file at once (its
dict and user lines, in the new order, where the first of them was;
the rest of the file stays). ~/.config/steno/config is a link into
this repo, so the change shows in git diff; the link stays a link.

OPTIONS

    -c FILE   settings (default ~/.config/steno/config)
    -p PORT   the serial port; auto (default) tries /dev/ttyACM0 to
              ttyACM9, every second until the keyboard is there
    -T        show the paper tape
    -S        show the suggestions
    -TS       show both (also -ST, or -T -S)
    -l X      what outline X gives in each dictionary, and how text X
              is written (shortest first)
    -a O T    add outline O -> T to the user dictionary; says first
              what O gives now and how T is written already
    -A        the same, asking for one outline and translation after
              another (empty outline: done). To stroke the outline,
              turn on raw mode (RA*U) and back off for the text.
    -t        test mode: one stroke or outline a line on stdin; prints
              B n (backspaces), S text, K combo
    -q        no status screen, even in a terminal
    -v        verbose: say each stroke
    -h        help

SETTINGS

One a line, # for comments (see config, which install.sh links to
~/.config/steno/config):

    port auto|PATH           the keyboard
    dictionaries DIR         where dict names are looked for
    user FILE [off]          a dictionary, and where -a and -A write;
                             made empty when it isn't there
    dict NAME [off]          a dictionary (.json, .modal, or
                             dotterel-modifiers.py); the first wins
    data DIR                 Dotterel's data (uk_to_us.json); default
                             DIR/../data of dictionaries
    undo N                   strokes * can undo (100)
    start_capitalized yes|no
    start_attached yes|no
    command NAME SHELL...    {PLOVER:NAME} runs SHELL with /bin/sh

The commands Plover itself knows: {PLOVER:TOGGLE}, RESUME, SUSPEND
(each also forgets what was typed, for undo) and QUIT.

A dictionary that changes on disk (steno -a, or an edit by hand) is
read again at the next stroke; one that can't be read keeps its old
contents.

PAPER TAPE AND SUGGESTIONS

Each is its own box, drawn as btop draws them: steno -T is the tape,
steno -S the suggestions, steno -TS both, side by side (one above the
other in a terminal under 60 columns). So they can go in one window
or in two, wherever there is room.

    +-+tape+--------------------------++-+suggestions+----------------+
    |         O     PB        on      ||on                            |
    |  T   H                  this    ||  -PB  AUPB  OPB              |
    |                                 ||on this                       |
    |                                 ||  THOPB                       |
    +---------------------------------++--+quit+-+clear+-+tape+-+sugg..+

The tape: each stroke in the steno order's columns and what it wrote
(* for a stroke that undid one). The suggestions: the last words, and
under them every outline that writes them, shortest first; the newest
at the bottom in both.

    q    quit (Ctrl+C, Ctrl+D too)
    t    show or hide the tape
    s    show or hide the suggestions
    c    clear both
    any other key draws it again

At least one box stays. Into a pipe, it writes the lines instead
(suggestions start with "= " when the tape is there too).

The engine sends them through two FIFOs, $XDG_STATE_HOME/steno/tape
and suggest (mode 0600, in a 0700 directory), each only while a viewer
has it open; with the suggestions hidden or not shown anywhere, it
doesn't look for them. One viewer of each at a time. Nothing is
written to disk. Whatever you type passes through them, so they are
yours only; don't show them where others see the screen.

PRIVACY

No network, no log file, no history kept on disk: what you typed lives
in memory for undo (100 strokes) and is gone when steno stops. -v
prints strokes to stderr; leave it off where stderr is kept.

KNOWN DIFFERENCES FROM PLOVER

    {=...} (Plover's custom transformers) and retro currency
        ({*($c)}) are not there.
    Upper and lower case of letters outside Latin-1, Latin
        Extended-A, Greek and Cyrillic are left as they are.
    A word longer than 500 bytes is cut.
    TOGGLE, RESUME and SUSPEND forget the history (Plover keeps it).

FILES

    main.c       settings, commands, the serial port, the main loop
    dict.c       strokes, dictionaries, modal and modifier dictionaries
    translate.c  Plover's translator: longest match, undo, macros
    format.c     Plover's formatter: spacing, case, metas
    ortho.c      orthography: a small regex engine and Plover's rules
    x11.c        the X protocol: XTEST, the keymap, spare keys
    tools.c      the tape and suggestion feeds, -l, -a, -A
    view.c       -T, -S, -TS: the boxes
    tui.c        the status screen
    screen.c     drawing boxes in the terminal, for both
    mkwords.c    builds words.c from words.txt at build time
    words.txt    Plover's American English word list (for orthography)
    test/        make test: a small corpus checked against Plover

LICENCE

steno is a port of Plover's code (https://github.com/openstenoproject/
plover), so it is under Plover's licence, the GNU GPL version 2 or
later. Plover's copyright belongs to its authors, who are named in
Plover's own sources; the port is by the owner of this repo. words.txt
is Plover's american_english_words.txt, unchanged.
